    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d859">

                <pb facs="0794" n="782" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg782"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111 A.R.–Subs/10</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Des Portes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1312</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-19">June 19,
                        1940</date>.<lb/>[Received June 24.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    circular telegram of June 13, 8 p.m., and to the Legation’s reply thereto No.
                    53, June 18, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="23" xml:id="d859-f1"/> I have the honor to
                    enclose herewith a copy and translation of the Decree signed by President Ubico
                    on June 17,<anchor n="24" xml:id="d859-f2"/> by which the entrance of
                    belligerent submarines into Guatemalan territorial waters is forbidden.</p>
                <p>The Department will note that the Decree declares Guatemalan territorial waters
                    to extend twelve sea miles from the low tide mark and to include the Bay of
                    Amatique.</p>
                <p>When I discussed this matter with the Foreign Minister,<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d859-f3"/> in accordance with the Department’s instructions he
                    stated that the recommendation of the Inter-American Neutrality Committee had
                    not been received by the Guatemalan Government, but he later informed me by
                    telephone that he had been mistaken in making this statement. From the comments
                    made to me by the Foreign Minister, it was quite evident that the Guatemalan
                    Government gladly acceded to the Department’s initiative.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fay Allen Des Portes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d859-f1" n="23" xml:id="d859-f1-content">
